+# Rolling upgrade: promote Rails 7.0, preview Rails 7.2
+
+**Deploy `Gemfile` / `Gemfile.lock`: Rails 7.0.10, Ruby 3.2.9, `load_defaults 7.0`.**
+The Ruby-only release (3.2.9 / Rails 6.1.7.10) is already deployed. Keep that
+**bridge artifact**, including its bundle, as the rollback target. The older
+3.0.7 / 6.1 release didn't have its readers. Do not skip the bridge.
+
+`Gemfile.next` / `Gemfile.next.lock` preview Rails 7.2.4 **on 7.2 defaults**, with
+the compatibility exceptions below. Do not deploy that bundle in this release.
+Both Gemfiles use the same Ruby, adapter selection, optional groups and plugin
+Gemfiles. The checked-in locks are for SQLite on Linux; resolve and test the
+lock for the production adapter/platform when building each artifact.
+
+## What crosses the boundary
+
+| Writer | Encrypted `_redmine_session` | `:mail_links` (key derivation / payload) | Shared cache writes |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Deployed 6.1 bridge (no `load_defaults`) | AES-256-CBC + HMAC, PBKDF2-SHA1, Marshal, no name purpose | SHA1 / Marshal | 6.1 |
+| Current 7.0 | AES-256-GCM, PBKDF2-SHA256, JSON, cookie-name purpose | SHA256 / Marshal | **6.1** |
+| Next 7.2 | Same as 7.0 | SHA256 / JSON | **7.0** |
+
+* **Cookies.** The 6.1 bridge can read the 7.0 writer. Both 7.x bundles keep
+  `cookies_serializer = :hybrid`: write JSON; accept authenticated legacy
+  Marshal. `config/initializers/rolling_upgrade.rb` keeps SHA1-derived read
+  keys for old signed cookies and CBC-encrypted sessions (Rails salts, key
+  lengths and 1,000 PBKDF2 iterations). Don't change the cookie name, scope,
+  purpose or session-token rows. Nested session hashes must accept string keys.
+  Database-backed autologin and password recovery tokens are not reissued.
+* **Mail links.** The salt is still `mail_links`; the change in digest above is
+  for PBKDF2, **not** the HMAC signing algorithm. Purpose and 30-day expiry
+  are still validated by Active Support. 7.0 must write Marshal for rollback
+  to 6.1. Its mailer verifier uses a Marshal writer with a JSON fallback on
+  read, so it can also read the next release's JSON. A same-key rotation alone
+  is insufficient: 7.0's plain Marshal reader raises on authenticated JSON.
+  Both releases retain the SHA1-derived reader for already-sent 6.1 mail.
+* **Temporary 7.2 override:** `use_message_serializer_for_metadata = false`.
+  Leave the 7.2 message payload serializer at its `:json_allow_marshal`
+  default. Write the older, dual-serialized metadata envelope: a 7.0 reader
+  that understands JSON payloads still cannot unpack the new `data` envelope.
+  Don't turn it on while rollback to 7.0 is possible.
+* **Shared caches:** 7.0 must keep `cache_format_version = 6.1` while 6.1 may
+  read its writes. 7.2 no longer supports writing 6.1; keep it writing 7.0
+  rather than 7.1, and marshalling Active Record instances in 6.1 format, for
+  the 7.0 reader. Expire or namespace incompatible old cache entries rather
+  than treating the cache as durable state.
+* **App behavior, not wire rotations:** keep `belongs_to_required_by_default =
+  false`; Redmine validates required associations itself. Existing serialized
+  database columns are YAML (including typed arrays/hashes; Role has its own
+  permissions coder). On 7.2 keep that column serializer, and let `Issue` run
+  commit callbacks on the first saved instance so parent changes are journaled.
+
+## Deploy and rehearse rollback
+
+1. Keep the **same effective `secret_key_base`** on every process and on both
+   sides. Preserve Redmine's generated `secret_token.rb` if used. Rails 7.2
+   no longer reads `secrets.yml`; if that is the source, provision its existing
+   value via credentials or `SECRET_KEY_BASE` before switching. Check effective
+   precedence if using both the generated initializer and an environment secret;
+   do not regenerate or log the key. Audit `additional_environment.rb`, plugins,
+   custom cookie salts and other verifier consumers separately.
+2. Test both bundles with the production database adapter and plugins on a
+   disposable database. For each `f` in `Gemfile` and `Gemfile.next`, run
+   `BUNDLE_GEMFILE=$f bundle install` then `BUNDLE_GEMFILE=$f bundle exec rails test test/integration/sessions_test.rb test/functional/mail_links_controller_test.rb`.
+   Rehearse with an already logged-in browser and a still-valid emailed link,
+   not just a fresh login. To exchange actual writer bytes in separate processes:
+
+   ```sh
+   export RAILS_ENV=test
+   d=$(mktemp -d)
+   for f in Gemfile Gemfile.next; do
+     BUNDLE_GEMFILE=$f bundle exec rails runner test/extra/rolling_upgrade_wire.rb write "$d/$f"
+   done
+   BUNDLE_GEMFILE=Gemfile bundle exec rails runner test/extra/rolling_upgrade_wire.rb read "$d/Gemfile.next"
+   BUNDLE_GEMFILE=Gemfile.next bundle exec rails runner test/extra/rolling_upgrade_wire.rb read "$d/Gemfile"
+   rm -r "$d"
+   ```
+
+3. Roll 7.0 out to **all** nodes before any 7.2 writer. After 7.0 has served
+   traffic roll back only to the deployed 6.1 bridge. After 7.2 has served
+   traffic roll back to **this 7.0 artifact**, not the earlier 7.0 preview
+   or 6.1: neither has the JSON mail reader. Rolling back code doesn't roll
+   back mail already delivered or cookies in dormant browsers.
+
+## Work for the next release
+
+* Promote the tested 7.2 bundle with its rollback overrides intact. Only
+  enable the new metadata envelope, cache and record marshalling formats once
+  every possible reader (including rollback) understands them. Keep readers
+  for the old formats when enabling new writers.
+* Keep the SHA1 mail reader at least 30 days after the **last** 6.1 write,
+  including rollbacks. Keep Marshal mail reads at least 30 days after the
+  last 7.0 write. Keep `:hybrid` and the legacy cookie rotations until no valid
+  old session can return; session cookies need not have a bounded lifetime.
+* Review application overrides separately. Rehearse migrations, plugins,
+  jobs, shared caches and non-SQLite adapters before production promotion.
+
+## Validation notes
+
+The previous release rehearsed Ruby 3.0 / 6.1 ↔ Ruby 3.2 / 6.1. For this
+promotion, both 7.x bundles booted with production eager loading. Separate
+processes exchanged signed cookies, encrypted sessions and mail links both
+ways for 6.1 ↔ 7.0 and 7.0 ↔ 7.2; 7.2 also read the dormant 6.1 values.
+Integration tests exercise the database-backed login on a real request;
+mail tests include wrong purpose, tampering and expiry.
+
+The two order-sensitive cases from the 6.1 deployment notes now specify the
+state they need: the menu test requests the board view, and the spent-hours
+test creates the child in the parent's project. File-store cache entries,
+including a cached Active Record instance, were also read across the rollback
+boundaries. The local checks used SQLite; configured SCM repositories, LDAP,
+browser system tests and production adapters/plugins still need deployment-site
+coverage.
+
+A login created on the 6.1 bridge also reached `/my/account` with session
+verification enabled after switching processes 6.1 → 7.0 → 6.1 → 7.0 → 7.2
+→ 7.0 against the same test database, without logging in again.
+
+Full SQLite suite: **5,311 runs, 0 failures, 0 errors, 41 skips** on each
+bundle (7.0 seed 38513; 7.2 seed 49319). Run date-relative fixture tests
+without crossing midnight UTC: one run did so, making three reminder bodies
+say “6 days late” rather than “5”; the mailer suite and full rerun passed.
